   499	## 6. Typed status and residual OPENs
   500	
   501	**Route 1 — `CLOSED-AS-A-ROUTE (negative)`, Prop. 2.3.** The transformation law
   502	requested by the registry is an identity (Prop. 2.1) and it consumes the Keller
   503	hypothesis completely. Any closure must be non-local. This is a proved negative,
   504	not an abandonment, and it is consistent with the gate review's withdrawal of
   505	the blanket "no valuative proof" claim: Theorem 3.5 *is* a Keller-specific
   506	theorem about the divisorial data, but it needs the global Euler identity and
   507	`pi_1`, not one valuation.
   508	
   509	**Route 2 — `CLOSED at N=4 under H2`; `OPEN` otherwise.** The split by `s_l`
   510	proposed in the registry turns out to be the wrong cut: the argument that works
   511	(Thm. 3.5) is insensitive to `s_l` and to the singularity type of the image,
   512	and runs through `chi_c` + [AMS] + meridian transitivity. The `s_l >= 2`
   513	`pi_1`-of-a-uniruled-complement computation the registry proposed is not needed.
   514	
   515	**Route 3 — `CLOSED at N=4 under H2`, Thm. 4.2.** The Domrina–Orevkov
   516	`mu=1` dismissal is now a numbered lemma (Prop. 4.1) with two independent
   517	proofs, and the mixed remainder `(mu=2)+(mu=1)` is settled under H2 (Thm. 4.2)
   518	and completely described without it (Thm. 4.3).
   519	
   520	> **`OPEN[B0-N4-REDUCIBLE-PI1]`.** The exact missing lemma is:
   521	>
   522	> *Let `D ⊂ C^2` be an irreducible affine plane curve whose normalisation is
   523	> `A^1` or `C^*` and all of whose affine branches are smooth. Is
   524	> `pi_1(C^2 - D)` abelian?*
   525	>
   526	> A YES closes B0 at `N=4` outright (via Thm. 4.3(3)), hence closes `A_F = B`
   527	> and the transposition horn with no H2 hypothesis. A weaker sufficient form
   528	> also suffices: *no surjection `pi_1(C^2-D) -> S_4` sends every meridian to a
   529	> transposition and the two local meridians at each double point to **disjoint**
   530	> transpositions* (Thm. 4.3(4)).
   531	>
   532	> Status of the literature on this lemma. Deligne–Fulton (Zariski's conjecture)
   533	> gives `pi_1(P^2 - C)` abelian for `C ⊂ P^2` irreducible **nodal**; Nori's
   534	> theorem gives abelianness under `C^2 > 2·#nodes`. Neither is quoted here
   535	> because (a) our `D` has one place at infinity, so `bar D ∪ L_∞` is never
   536	> nodal and the affine complement is not the projective one, and (b) our double
   537	> points are only known to be two *smooth* branches, which permits tangency.
   538	> **Neither PDF was acquired in this lane; nothing above uses them.** This is a
   539	> pure acquisition-and-check item, not a research question.
   540	
   541	> **`OPEN[B0-GENERAL-N]`.** For `N >= 5`, B0 is open even under H2. The reason
   542	> is exactly located: Cor. 3.7 needs all `corr_l = 0` to fire, and at `N >= 5`
   543	> the budget leaves room for a trivial dicritical *plus* a correction. Concretely
   544	> at `N=5` under H2, exactly two profiles carrying a trivial dicritical survive
   545	> everything proved here: `(mu,corr) = (2,0)+(1,1)` and `(2,1)+(1,0)`. Relaxing
   546	> Lemma 3.3 to `nu >= s - sum_l corr_l` and rerunning (3.2) gives
   547	> `2a - N - 1 + (N-2a)s + sum_p a_p <= 0` in the one-correction case, which is
