+# B0-ALL-N: eta-criticality at every degree
+
+## 0. Scope, source control, and verdict
+
+The three frozen inputs were hashed before they were read.  `shasum -a 256`
+returned, in the order in the charge,
+
+```text
+67a9482eecd3fcb5f06bcf2967d4b73f5761429b5be597f2809b8e0767b1b02c
+b37ec3bfd41eb22c14e29b06284100a61c50fd849150fefe2eab89e1d1a78c55
+bbd48de1b028f6c71f006c3f27c10a3b96c593e6da58088c8393a992bde4a963
+```
+
+All three match.  Below, `N5`, `B0`, and `Coord` mean respectively the
+three frozen files in the order displayed in the prompt.
+
+**Verdict.**  The proposed theorem `B0-H2-ALL-N` does not follow from the
+promoted eta-criticality mechanism.  The exact surviving case is not a
+multiple-point ambiguity: it is ramification in a nonprimitive intermediate
+cover.  The sharp result proved here is:
+
+> If (A_F=D) is irreducible and a dicritical has (mu=1), then
+> (widetilde D\simeq\mathbb A^1), that dicritical maps isomorphically to
+> (widetilde D), and the normalization map
+> (eta:\widetilde D\to D) is immersive everywhere.  Every correction,
+> after passing to the parametrization point supplied by [Z-6.5(b)], is
+> therefore ramification of a cover (h_l:\mathbb A^1\to\mathbb A^1) with
+> (mu_l\ge2) and (s_l\ge2).  Moreover the generic affine-fibre count
+> (a) satisfies (2a>N), hence necessarily (N\ge11).
+
+Thus `B0-H2` is proved by this packet for every (N\le10), and for every
+(N) if all correction-bearing normalization covers have degree one.  The
+all-degree remainder is
+`OPEN[B0-H2-N>=11/RAMIFIED-NONPRIMITIVE-COVER]`.  No statement below is an
+attainment claim or a new exit-price assertion.
+
+## 1. Promoted licenses actually available
+
+Let (l) be an affine-image dicritical,
+(phi_l:l'\to D_l), (s_l=\deg\phi_l), and
+
+\[
+ \operatorname{corr}_l=
+ \sum_x(\mu_x-\mu_l)\ge0.
+\]
+
+These are the definitions fixed at `B0:38--50`.  The exact Orevkov budget is
+
+\[
+ \sum_l(\mu_l+\operatorname{corr}_l)=N-1                         \tag{1.1}
+\]
+
+(`B0:63--65`).  `Coord:24--48` promotes the branch-locus and normalization
+package, H2 strictness
+
+\[
+ A_F\text{ irreducible}\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
+ \sum_l\mu_l\le N-2,
+ \qquad \sum_l\operatorname{corr}_l\ge1,                         \tag{1.2}
+\]
+
+and the pointwise smooth-branch reading.  Under H2 every (D_l) is the same
+curve (D=A_F), as used explicitly in the proof at `B0:302--310`.  At a
+generic point of (D), promoted flatness gives
+
+\[
+ a+\sum_l s_l\mu_l=N,qquad a\ge1                               \tag{1.3}
+\]
+
+(`B0:182--188,200--211`; promotion at `Coord:34--38`).
+
+The pointwise implications must be kept in their exact directions.
+[O-5.2] says no jump implies that the local restriction is a nonsingular
+embedding (`B0:66--70`).  Its contrapositive is *non-embedding implies a
+jump*, not the direction needed below.  The required equivalence is
+[Z-6.5(b)]:
+
+\[
+ \mu_x>\mu_l\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad d\phi_l=0,
+ \qquad \mu_l=1\quad\Longrightarrow\quad d\phi_l\ne0
+ \text{ everywhere}.                                           \tag{1.4}
+\]
+
+Here “singular point of the immersed curve” means criticality of this
+parametrization; intersections of smooth branches are expressly excluded.
+This is the binding correction at `Coord:43--45,108--116`, and is also
+spelled out at `N5:55--64`.  In particular, (1.4) does **not** say that the
+reduced image germ is unibranch or singular.
+
+Two primary PDFs were checked directly and rehashed:
+
+* S. Yu. Orevkov, *On three-sheeted polynomial mappings of
+  \(\mathbb C^2\)*, `refs/jc86.pdf`, SHA-256
+  `f80d4a7d7e04987ce7dece58f33cff20ea9210183ca3ffd4488f39a2147532db`.
+  Lemma 2.1 is on PDF p. 2; Lemma 5.2 is on pp. 8--9.
+* H. Zoladek, *An application of Newton--Puiseux charts to the Jacobian
+  problem*, `refs/zoladek2008_official.pdf`, SHA-256
+  `88d5a35414ad11ffc96e32551810ef773e88be2db12ce39478c964cb602149ad`.
+  Proposition 6.5(b) and its definition of immersed-curve singularity are on
+  printed/PDF pp. 457--458.
+
+Orevkov Lemma 2.1 is valid independently of (N): every connected finite-image
+boundary chain has one (L_F)-component (l\simeq\mathbb P^1), meeting
+(L_\infty) at exactly one point.  Hence
+
+\[
+ l'=l\cap\Phi^{-1}(\mathbb A^2)\simeq\mathbb P^1-\{\infty\}
+ \simeq\mathbb A^1.                                             \tag{1.5}
+\]
+
+This is the primary-source affine-line repair consumed by `Coord:56--66`;
+`N5:81--91,164--179` uses the same lemma and the finite normalization
+factorization.  I use (1.5) at its primary-source, all-degree scope, not as an
+extrapolation from the (N=5) values (s_l=1).
+
+## 2. Normalization factorization and the trivial dicritical
+
+Let (A=\mathbb C[D]), let \(\bar A\) be its normalization in
+(K(D)), and write \(\mathbb C[l']=\mathbb C[t]\).  Dominance of
+(phi_l) embeds (K(D)) in (mathbb C(t)).  An element of \(\bar A\)
+is integral over (A), hence its image is integral over (mathbb C[t]);
+normality of (mathbb C[t]) puts that image in (mathbb C[t]).  Thus the
+normalization universal property gives a unique global factorization
+
+\[
+ \phi_l=\eta\circ h_l,qquad
+ h_l:l'\longrightarrow\widetilde D.                              \tag{2.1}
+\]
+
+This construction is over all of (l'), including points mapping to
+(\operatorname{Sing}D): (h_l(x)) is the particular normalization place
+selected by the source germ, not the physical target point.
+
+Choose a target coordinate whose pullback to (mathbb A^1) is a nonconstant
+polynomial (f(t)).  Then (mathbb C[t]) is finite over
+(mathbb C[f(t)]), and the same module generators work over the intermediate
+ring \(\bar A\).  Hence (h_l) is finite.  It is dominant, so its closed image
+is all of (widetilde D), and
+
+\[
+ \deg h_l=[\mathbb C(t):K(D)]=s_l.                               \tag{2.2}
+\]
+
+This proves the precise finiteness/surjectivity license that is used without
+proof in `B0:236--243` and in the degree-one instance `N5:81--91`.
+
+Now suppose (mu_0=1).  By (1.4),
+(operatorname{corr}_0=0) and (d\phi_0(x)\ne0) for every (x\in l'_0).
+The chain rule
+
+\[
+ d\phi_0(x)=d\eta_{h_0(x)}\circ dh_0(x)                          \tag{2.3}
+\]
+
+forces both (dh_0(x)\ne0) and (d\eta_{h_0(x)}\ne0).  Since (h_0) is
+surjective, (eta) is immersive at **every** normalization point, including
+every point over (\operatorname{Sing}D).  Also (h_0) is a connected finite
+etale cover of degree (s_0).
+
+In fact H3 is not needed.  By (1.5) its source is (mathbb A^1).  Extend
+(h_0) to smooth projective completions
+(ar h_0:\mathbb P^1\to\bar D).  Every puncture of (widetilde D) has a
+preimage at the sole omitted source point, so (ar D-\widetilde D) is one
+point and its full preimage is ({\infty\}).  Riemann--Hurwitz first gives
+(ar D\simeq\mathbb P^1).  All ramification of (ar h_0) would then be
+at infinity, where its contribution is (s_0-1), whereas Riemann--Hurwitz
+requires (2s_0-2).  Thus (s_0=1), (h_0) is an isomorphism, and
+
+\[
+ \widetilde D\simeq\mathbb A^1.                                 \tag{2.4}
+\]
+
+If one deliberately withholds Orevkov Lemma 2.1, the safe conclusion without
+H3 stops at “connected finite etale (h_0)”; H3 would then trivialize it.
+At the actual licensed scope, (1.5) proves (2.4), so existence of a trivial
+dicritical itself forces H3.
+
+## 3. What a correction point really forces
+
+By (1.2), some correction summand is positive.  A correction is recorded at a
+point of Orevkov's collapsed/physical model, whereas (h_l) is a map from the
+normal parametrizing curve.  I do not identify those objects.  Instead pass to
+the parametrization point (widehat x\in l') associated to that summand by the
+pointwise [Z-6.5(b)] statement, and put (z=h_l(\widehat x)).  Then
+
+\[
+ 0=d\phi_l(\widehat x)
+   =d\eta_z\circ dh_l(\widehat x).                                 \tag{3.1}
+\]
+
+Section 2 proved (d\eta_z\ne0) at every (z).  Therefore
+
+\[
+ dh_l(\widehat x)=0.                                                \tag{3.2}
+\]
+
+This is the opposite of the proposed eta-criticality conclusion: the
+criticality is forced into the intermediate cover.  Conversely, at any source
+point where (dh_l=0), (2.3) makes (d\phi_l=0), and [Z-6.5(b)] gives a jump.
+Thus, at the parametrization-point level licensed by [Z], correction support is
+exactly ramification support of (h_l).  It follows that every
+correction-bearing (h_l) has (s_l\ge2); it also has (mu_l\ge2), because
+(mu_l=1) makes its parametrization immersive everywhere.
+
+The image-germ alternatives are now exact.  A reduced branch of (D) at
+(p) is singular precisely when (d\eta_z=0) at its normalization place.
+That alternative has been excluded.  Hence every branch of (D) is smooth,
+and any singular point of (D) is multibranch (tangent smooth branches are
+allowed).  This agrees with promoted Lemma 3.3 (`B0:219--225`): that lemma says
+zero correction implies a smooth branch; it does not say that a correction
+implies a singular reduced branch.
+
+Nor does a multiple point itself explain (3.1).  If (h_l) is unramified at a
+place over a multiple point, the selected branch parametrization is immersive
+and there is no jump.  If (h_l) ramifies there, the jump comes from (h_l).
+Indeed a correction image need not be a reduced singular point at all: locally,
+(eta(u)) may parametrize a smooth branch while
+(h_l(t)=u_0+t^e), (e\ge2).  Then (eta\circ h_l) is critical although its
+reduced image is smooth.  This is exactly the nonprimitive loophole warned of
+at `N5:60--64`; it is a local diagnostic, not a Keller-map witness.
+
+## 4. Sharp theorem and proof
+
+The preceding argument already proves a cover-theoretic dichotomy.  The
+promoted Euler identity makes its numerical scope substantially sharper.
+
+> **Theorem 4.1 (sharp eta/cover obstruction under H2).**  Let (F) be a
+> noninvertible plane Keller map of geometric degree (N), and assume
+> (D=A_F) is irreducible.  If an affine-image dicritical has (mu_0=1), then:
+>
+> 1. (s_0=1), (h_0:l'_0\overset\sim\longrightarrow\widetilde D), and
+>    (widetilde D\simeq\mathbb A^1); in particular H3 follows rather than
+>    being an extra hypothesis;
+> 2. (eta:\widetilde D\to D) is immersive everywhere, so all reduced
+>    branches of (D) are smooth and all singularities of (D) are
+>    multibranch;
+> 3. some other dicritical has (mu_l\ge2), (s_l\ge2), and a correction;
+>    every correction is supported, after the [Z] point lift, on ramification
+>    of its (h_l:\mathbb A^1\to\mathbb A^1);
+> 4. (2a>N) and (N\ge11).
+>
+> Consequently no (mu=1) dicritical exists for (N\le10), or at any degree
+> if every correction-bearing (h_l) has degree one.
+
+*Proof.*  Parts 1--3 are §§2--3 together with strictness (1.2).  For part 4,
+let
+
+\[
+ \Sigma=\operatorname{Sing}D,\quad \sigma=|\Sigma|,\quad
+ ν=\sum_{p\in\Sigma}(|\eta^{-1}(p)|-1),\quad
+ A_\Sigma=\sum_{p\in\Sigma}a_p.
+\]
+
+The irreducible curve (D) is singular by promoted Corollary 3.6
+(`Coord:39--43`), so (sigma\ge1).  Since all its branches are smooth, every
+singularity is multibranch and (ν\ge\sigma).  From
+(widetilde D\simeq\mathbb A^1),
+
+\[
+ \chi_c(D-\Sigma)=1-\sigma-ν.
+\]
+
+The promoted irreducible Euler identity, re-derived at
+`B0:236--265` and stated at `N5:181--188`, is
+
+\[
+ (N-a)\chi_c(D-\Sigma)=N-1-N\sigma+A_\Sigma.
+\]
+
+Substitution and regrouping give the exact equality
+
+\[
+ 0=(a-1)+(N-2a)\sigma+(N-a)(ν-\sigma)+A_\Sigma.                  \tag{4.1}
+\]
+
+If (2a\le N), every term is nonnegative; since (N\ge3) in the
+noninvertible scope and (sigma\ge1), either the second term is positive or,
+when (N=2a), (a-1>0).  This contradicts (4.1), so (2a>N).
+
+Finally (1.3) contains the contribution (s_0\mu_0=1), while the correction
+carrier from part 3 contributes at least (2\cdot2=4).  Hence
+
+\[
+ N=a+\sum_l s_l\mu_l\ge a+5>N/2+5,
+\]
+
+so (N>10).  The conditional degree-one assertion follows because strictness
+requires a correction, whereas §3 requires its cover degree to be at least
+two.  \(\square\)
+
+The first arithmetically possible stratum illustrates exactly where the proof
+stops.  If (N=11), the two inequalities force (a=6), and (1.3) leaves
+exactly
+
+\[
+ (\mu,s,\operatorname{corr})=(1,1,0)+(2,2,7).                    \tag{4.2}
+\]
+
+Equation (4.1) further requires
+
+\[
+ \sigma=5+5(ν-\sigma)+A_\Sigma,                                 \tag{4.3}
+\]
+
+so in particular at least five multibranch affine singularities.  Packet
+(4.2)--(4.3) is only a necessary, aggregate consistency stratum; it is not an
+attainment claim and not a Keller example.  No promoted statement in the
+charged inputs excludes its ramified quadratic (h_l).  Closing it, or proving
+the missing general inequality (2a\le N), would go beyond the present
+mechanism (compare the named `T3.5-SHARPEN` successor at `B0:584--586`).
+
+## 5. Consequences and remaining gaps by degree
+
+### 5.1 Irreducible (A_F)
+
+At (N=5), `Coord:69--74` promotes the sole H2 profile containing a trivial
+dicritical,
+
+\[
+ (\mu,\operatorname{corr})=(2,1)+(1,0),\qquad
+ (s_2,s_1,a)=(1,1,2).
+\]
+
+Both (h)'s have degree one, so the correction cannot be absorbed in a cover;
+this is precisely the common-normalization contradiction proved at
