+The irreducible curve \(D\) is singular by promoted Corollary 3.6 (**Coord:39--43**), so \(\sigma\ge1\). Since all branches are smooth, every singularity is multibranch and \(\nu\ge\sigma\). From \(\widetilde D\simeq\mathbb A^1\),
+
+\[
+ \chi_c(D-\Sigma)=1-\sigma-\nu.
+\]
+
+The promoted irreducible Euler identity, re-derived at **B0:236--265** and stated at **N5:181--188**, is
+
+\[
+ (N-a)\chi_c(D-\Sigma)=N-1-N\sigma+A_\Sigma.
+\]
+
+Substitution and regrouping give
+
+\[
+ 0=(a-1)+(N-2a)\sigma+(N-a)(\nu-\sigma)+A_\Sigma.                \tag{4.1}
+\]
+
+If \(2a\le N\), all four terms are nonnegative. Since \(N\ge3\) in the noninvertible scope and \(\sigma\ge1\), either the second term is positive or, when \(N=2a\), \(a-1>0\). This contradicts (4.1), so \(2a>N\).
+
+Finally (1.3) contains the contribution \(s_0\mu_0=1\), while the correction carrier in part 3 contributes at least \(2\cdot2=4\). Hence
+
+\[
+ N=a+\sum_l s_l\mu_l\ge a+5>N/2+5,
+\]
+
+so \(N>10\). The conditional degree-one assertion follows because strictness requires a correction, whereas §3 requires its cover degree to be at least two. This proves the theorem.
+
+The first arithmetically possible stratum shows exactly where the proof stops. At \(N=11\), the two inequalities force \(a=6\), and (1.3) leaves exactly
+
+\[
+ (\mu,s,\operatorname{corr})=(1,1,0)+(2,2,7).                    \tag{4.2}
+\]
+
+Equation (4.1) further requires
+
+\[
+ \sigma=5+5(\nu-\sigma)+A_\Sigma,                               \tag{4.3}
+\]
+
+so there must be at least five multibranch affine singularities. Packet (4.2)--(4.3) is only a necessary aggregate stratum. It is not an attainment claim and not a Keller example. No promoted statement in the charged inputs excludes its ramified quadratic \(h_l\). Closing it, or proving the missing general inequality \(2a\le N\), goes beyond this mechanism; compare the named T3.5-SHARPEN successor at **B0:584--586**.
